**Blockers: 3 · Concerns: 7**

**Strongest hit (ENN-H1)** — the filter paragraph in H2 6: *"'You are not dominating' is a claim about output, and output is a number she is already grading more harshly than Swoopes ever could, so it bounces… 'You are a bad person' cannot be handled that way, because the audit is the moral instrument."* It's the one place the type does falsifiable work — it predicts which criticisms she absorbs and which she goes on television about, and the record matches. It's also the site's own Type 1 core fear ("Am I good?") rendered as observable behavior.

**Highest-priority concern** is actually a blocker, ENN-R1: the article's centerpiece — that Clark grades correctness at the moment of decision and treats the outcome as somebody else's business — is invented. She has explained her turnovers herself, and describes a different mechanism: *"maybe it's a toxic part of me, but I always think it's going to go well, and I throw it and it doesn't go well,"* plus *"that's just how I play."* That's miscalibrated outcome optimism she names as a flaw and keeps, not outcome-indifference. The packet had this only at headline level (S-29); I ran it down.

The other two blockers: the Rabbit Hole's subtype call contradicts 9takes' own Type 1 pillar (it rules out the sexual instinct by defining it as "intensity toward a single rival," then rebuts Type 8 — which the pillar names as precisely the one-to-one One's standard mistype), and all three One-vs-Three tiebreakers are produced by trimming the half of each quote that points to Three, including a silent mid-quote excision of "I want to be the best player in the world."

Two notes for whoever edits: the fix for the weak counterargument section (ENN-C6 — it rebuts an image-managing Three, never the sp-Three that actually competes) is already written inside the article, in the H2 6 filter. And I closed the packet's open question on Type 6 without research — the draft is right to skip it.
